Q1. What is a Class?

Interview Ready Answer

A class is a user-defined blueprint or template used to create objects.
It defines the state (fields/instance variables) and behavior (methods)
that every object of that class will have.

Key Points:

• Class is a logical entity.
• Defines state and behavior.
• One class can create multiple objects.
• Memory for instance variables is allocated only when an object is created.

Example:

class Student{
    String name;
    int age;

    void display(){}
}

======================================================================

Q2. What is an Object?

Interview Ready Answer

An object is an instance of a class. It represents an entity having
its own state, behavior and identity.

Key Points:

• Created using new keyword.
• Occupies Heap Memory.
• Each object has its own copy of instance variables.
• Multiple objects can be created from one class.

Example:

Student s1 = new Student();
Student s2 = new Student();

======================================================================

Q3. What happens internally when we execute

Student s = new Student();

Interview Ready Answer

Step 1
JVM loads Student class (if not already loaded).

↓

Step 2
Heap memory is allocated.

↓

Step 3
Instance variables get default values.

↓

Step 4
Constructor executes.

↓

Step 5
Constructor initializes the object.

↓

Step 6
Reference is returned.

↓

Step 7
Reference variable s (stored in Stack) points to the object in Heap.

======================================================================

Q4. Can a class exist without an object?

Interview Ready Answer

Yes.

A class can exist without creating any object.

When JVM loads a class, its metadata is stored in Method Area
(Metaspace). Objects are created later only when the new keyword
is used.

Example:

class Student{

    static void display(){

    }
}

Student.display();

No object is created.

======================================================================

Q5. Difference between Class and Object.

Interview Ready Answer

Class

• Blueprint
• Logical entity
• Defines state and behavior
• Loaded into Metaspace
• Can exist without objects
• One class can create many objects

Object

• Instance of class
• Runtime entity
• Stores actual data
• Allocated in Heap
• Cannot exist without class
• Every object has its own state

