Q6. What is a Constructor? Why do we need it?

Interview Ready Answer

A constructor is a special member of a class that is automatically
invoked when an object is created.

Purpose

• Initialize object.
• Assign initial values.
• Prepare object for use.

Properties

• Same name as class.
• No return type.
• Automatically called.
• Executes once for every object.

Types

1. No-Argument Constructor

Student(){

}

2. Parameterized Constructor

Student(String name,int age){

    this.name=name;
    this.age=age;
}

======================================================================

Q7. Difference between Constructor and Method.

Interview Ready Answer

Constructor

• Initializes object.
• Same name as class.
• No return type.
• Automatically invoked.
• Executes once per object.
• Cannot be overridden.
• Cannot be inherited.
• Can be overloaded.

Method

• Defines behavior.
• Any valid name.
• Can return value or be void.
• Called explicitly.
• Can execute multiple times.
• Can be inherited.
• Can be overridden.
• Can be overloaded.

======================================================================

Q8. If Java already provides a default constructor,
why do developers write their own constructors?

Interview Ready Answer

Developers write constructors to:

• Initialize object with required values.
• Validate input.
• Assign default values.
• Support constructor overloading.
• Ensure object is always in a valid state.

Important Point

If the programmer writes any constructor,
Java DOES NOT generate the default constructor.

Example

class Student{

    Student(String name){

    }

}

Now,

new Student();

Compilation Error

because default constructor is not generated.

